on-irrelevant\"}, \"per_workload\": [{\"uuid\": \"469b2796-deb3-5a83-a7a6-1b2f84b86f20\", \"num_tokens\": 2240, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 1.82329648134368, \"max_abs\": 4.589557647705078e-06, \"max_rel\": 5.927932306803996e-06}, {\"uuid\": \"993dd417-9fb2-5d66-9b81-c1e15a113463\", \"num_tokens\": 2272, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 1.7268643641808477, \"max_abs\": 2.682209014892578e-06, \"max_rel\": 4.065022039867472e-06}, {\"uuid\": \"0fc8f5f9-1900-55f4-b7d8-acefecbead69\", \"num_tokens\": 6144, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 2.349381086686342, \"max_abs\": 3.159046173095703e-06, \"max_rel\": 4.936009645462036e-06}, {\"uuid\": \"d6d0eb83-de2e-5a6d-ba23-ee72ae216708\", \"num_tokens\": 2048, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 1.3321746028649644, \"max_abs\": 2.205371856689453e-06, \"max_rel\": 3.500590310068219e-06}, {\"uuid\": \"1d554a6c-1d9d-54c7-9ed9-6d4b50c95906\", \"num_tokens\": 2112, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 1.6773355209356895, \"max_abs\": 16.0, \"max_rel\": 0.13114753365516663}], \"all_correct\": true, \"geomean_speedup\": 1.7524676779181187, \"arithmetic_mean_speedup\": 1.7818104112023048, \"decision\": \"champion\", \"decision_reason\": \"NEW CHAMPION: 5/5 PASSED, geomean 1.7525x (vs c010 0.8592x) -- a 2.04x improvement from the single bf16-HMMA revert, confirming the GEMM was the dominant cost and that bf16 tensor cores are both faithful (within the 0.99 match ratio) and far faster. NOTE: workload 5 now shows max_abs=16.0, max_rel=0.131 -- bf16 rounding flips a borderline expert selection on a SMALL fraction of tokens (index-difference 16), but the >=0.99 match ratio absorbs it (still PASSED); the other 4 workloads are ~exact (max_abs ~2-5e-6). This is a real but tolerated correctness margin to watch: further precision reduction is NOT advisable, but bf16 as-is is safe. Now GEMM-tiling-bound: c011's GEMM is deliberately tiny/conservative (BLOCK_M=16, BLOCK_K=32, num_stages=1, num_warps=4) from the debugging phase -- lots of headroom. c012 raises the GEMM tiling for better tensor-core utilization and latency hiding.\", \"cumulative_evals\": 11, \"skills_used\": \"none\", \"next_candidate_plan\": \"c012 (parent c011): GEMM tiling step -- BLOCK_M=32, BLOCK_K=64, num_stages=2, num_warps=8 (double-buffered, wider K, more warps). Note B-tile [64,256] bf16 x2 stages ~72KB > 48KB static cap, so this also tests Triton's dynamic-shared-memory opt-in on this A800 stack (expected to work; the c001-c006 RUNTIME_ERRORs were the python-list bug, proven by c007, NOT shared memory). If c012 faster+correct -> continue tiling (BLOCK_M=64, num_stages=3). If c012 RUNTIME_ERRORs -> opt-in is the limiter; fall back to a <=48KB config (BLOCK_M=64, BLOCK_K=32, num_stages=2). Later: re-fuse the two kernels to drop the logits[T,256] DRAM round-trip + second launch.\"}","+{\"candidate\": \"c012\", \"parent\": \"c011\", \"source_sha256\": \"7644bf21b236a1b7f7a70340fbad0eb7c0d36764b388db7475ff9f533b56e14e\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-18T01:19:30Z\", \"hypothesis\": \"Runtime is GEMM-bound and c011's GEMM tiling was tiny (BLOCK_M=16,BLOCK_K=32,stages=1,warps=4). Enlarging to BLOCK_M=32, BLOCK_K=64, num_stages=2, num_warps=8 (double-buffered, wider K, more warps) improves tensor-core utilization and hides load latency; correctness-neutral (bf16 logits identical up to fp32 summation order). Also tests Triton's dynamic-shared-memory opt-in (>48KB) on this A800 stack.\", \"config\": {\"arch\": \"two-kernel split\", \"gemm\": {\"BLOCK_M\": 32, \"BLOCK_K\": 64, \"num_warps\": 8, \"num_stages\": 2, \"precision\": \"bf16 HMMA fp32-accum\"}, \"route\": {\"grid\": \"(T,)\", \"num_warps\": 4, \"num_stages\": 1}, \"output_order\": \"torch.topk(sorted=False)\", \"sigmoid\": \"1/(1+exp(-x))\", \"sentinel\": \"-1e30\"}, \"validation\": {\"static_gate_passed\": true, \"static_gate_notes\": \"only Kernel A launch tiling changed vs c011 (BLOCK_M 16->32, BLOCK_K 32->64, stages 1->2, warps 4->8); routing kernel byte-identical; perf-only, correctness-neutral\"}, \"per_workload\": [{\"uuid\": \"469b2796-deb3-5a83-a7a6-1b2f84b86f20\", \"num_tokens\": 2240, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 3.3758469509764844, \"max_abs\": 4.589557647705078e-06, \"max_rel\": 5.927932306803996e-06}, {\"uuid\": \"993dd417-9fb2-5d66-9b81-c1e15a113463\", \"num_tokens\": 2272, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 3.1749319736229253, \"max_abs\": 2.682209014892578e-06, \"max_rel\": 4.065022039867472e-06}, {\"uuid\": \"0fc8f5f9-1900-55f4-b7d8-acefecbead69\", \"num_tokens\": 6144, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 3.6541397481051026, \"max_abs\": 3.159046173095703e-06, \"max_rel\": 4.936009645462036e-06}, {\"uuid\": \"d6d0eb83-de2e-5a6d-ba23-ee72ae216708\", \"num_tokens\": 2048, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 2.5918563050001775, \"max_abs\": 2.205371856689453e-06, \"max_rel\": 3.500590310068219e-06}, {\"uuid\": \"1d554a6c-1d9d-54c7-9ed9-6d4b50c95906\", \"num_tokens\": 2112, \"status\": \"PASSED\", \"correct\": true, \"speedup\": 3.2832179642952575, \"max_abs\": 16.0, \"max_rel\": 0.13114753365516663}], \"all_correct\": true, \"geomean_speedup\": 3.1956749052864817, \"arithmetic_mean_speedup\": 3.2159985883999895, \"decision\": \"champion\", \"decision_reason\": \"NEW CHAMPION: 5/5 PASSED, geomean 3.1957x (vs c011 1.7525x) -- a 1.82x further improvement from GEMM tiling alone, confirming (a) GEMM-bound thesis and (b) the >48KB dynamic-shared-memory opt-in works on this A800 stack (no RUNTIME_ERROR at BLOCK_K=64/stages=2), definitively closing the c005-era shared-memory red herring. Correctness margins identical to c011 (workload 5 max_abs=16.0/max_rel=0.131 tolerated by 0.99 match ratio; others ~exact) since logits are numerically unchanged. Still likely GEMM-bound with headroom: BLOCK_M can go to 64, K-loop can pipeline deeper (num_stages=3), and the two-kernel split still pays a logits[T,256] DRAM write+read + a second launch. c013 pushes tiling further (BLOCK_M=64, num_stages=3) to find the tensor-core/occupancy sweet spot before attempting the bigger structural win (re-fusion).\", \"cumulative_evals\": 12, \"skills_used\": \"none\", \"next_candidate_plan\": \"c013 (parent c012): GEMM tiling BLOCK_M=64, BLOCK_K=64, num_stages=3, num_warps=8 (deeper pipeline, more tokens/program). Watch for spill/occupancy regression vs c012; if faster keep, if slower revert to c012 tiling and pivot to re-fusion (single fused GEMM+routing kernel) to remove the logits DRAM round-trip and second launch.\"}"]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false}}
